# Line endings, decided here rather than left to whoever cloned it.
#
# A .sh stored with CRLF fails on Linux at the very first line - the shebang
# becomes "/usr/bin/env bash\r", and what you get back is
#
#     bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M: No such file or directory
#
# which names neither the file's line endings nor anything you could act on.
# Sourced files are worse: they run, and the trailing \r ends up inside string
# comparisons, so the failures are silent and make no sense.
#
# These files are checked out on Windows, where git's autocrlf would otherwise
# put CRLF into them and then commit it back.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.ovpn          text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf

# These only ever run on Windows, where CRLF is what the tooling expects.
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# Prose, left to the platform.
*.md            text
